[INFO] [validation] WARNING: Outline drifted from user-specified sections: expected 8 headings, matched 0. Generated: A₀ as First Actuality; In Physics III.1, Aristotle defines κίνησις as the actuality (ἐντελέχεια) of the potential qua potential — a formulation that has generated centuries of interpretive difficulty precisely because it appears to locate motion in an ontological space between pure potentiality and completed actuality. Heidegger, commenting on the passage at Physics 201a9–15, characterizes it as the "genuine definition of movement" and notes that it rests upon the recognition that the categories themselves admit of a determinate "twofoldness" (διχῶς), a capacity to be "thus-and-so" that constitutes "the ontological condition of the fact that the beings that are determined by these categories are possible beings in movement" (Heidegger, *Basic Concepts of Aristotelian Philosophy*, p. 210). The twofoldness to which Heidegger draws attention is the dual character of every being that falls under one of the relevant categories: it is both what it actually is and what it is capable of becoming, and motion is the actualization of the latter aspect precisely as that aspect. Subsequently, this definition demands careful unpacking if we are to see why motion constitutes an energeia ateles — an incomplete actuality. The crucial point is that motion, unlike a complete actuality such as seeing or thinking, does not possess its end simultaneously with its exercise. As Aristotle notes in the Metaphysics, "where the result is something apart from the exercise, the actuality is in the thing that is being made, e.g. the act of building is in the thing that is being built and that of weaving in the thing that is being woven, and similarly in all other cases, and in general the movement is in the thing that is being moved" (Aristotle, *Metaphysics*, p. 110). Motion is always directed toward an end it has not yet achieved; it is energeia, certainly, but an energeia that is constitutively incomplete, always on the way toward a terminus it does not yet possess. Hence it is "incomplete" not in the sense of being deficient but in the sense of being structurally open: its very being consists in the not-yet-having-arrived. This structural incompleteness is what makes motion so foundational for the actualization chain. If motion were simply a completed state, it could not serve as the ongoing medium through which potentialities are progressively actualized. Burke captures the temporal dimension of this point when he describes "the continuity of motion (or 'duration') which we instinctively sense" as something that "is analyzed by intellectual concepts into a succession of disconnected steps or stages, as the movement of the actors in a motion picture is photographed by a series of stills, with an unphotographed hiatus between each exposure and the next" (Burke, *A Grammar of Motives*, pp. 431–433). Burke's analogy illuminates the challenge inherent in conceptualizing motion: it is continuous, but our conceptual apparatus tends to break it into discrete moments. Aristotle's definition resists this discretization by insisting that motion is a single, continuous actuality — the actuality of the potential as such — and thus cannot be reduced to a series of completed states. Furthermore, Heidegger's reading deepens the ontological significance of this incompleteness. 213–215). The point is that ἐνέργεια was originally at home in the domain of motion before it was extended to designate the fully completed actualities of substance and form. Motion is thus, in a genetic sense, the primary locus of actuality — the domain from which the very concept of actuality was drawn. For the purposes of our actualization chain, this means that A₀ is not merely temporally prior but conceptually foundational: the notion of actuality that governs the entire chain from A₁ through A₂ has its origin in the incomplete actuality of motion itself. Indeed, it is precisely because motion is an incomplete actuality that it can serve as the ground for a chain — it is the ongoing process within which further actualities emerge.; Priority of Actuality and the Chain Structure; The Relational Structure of Kinesis; Chronos as the Number of Motion
